const e=[{id:"sv-5-201",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In 5S, the Japanese word **Seiton** corresponds to which English step?",options:["Sorting","Straightening (set in order)","Shining","Sustaining"],answer:1,hint:"Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke = Sort, Straighten, Shine, Standardize, Sustain — in that order.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The five Japanese words follow the five English steps in the same order: Seiri → Sorting, **Seiton → Straightening**, Seiso → Shining, Seiketsu → Standardizing, Shitsuke → Sustaining."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Seiton is the second word, so it is the second S: *a place for everything and everything in its place* — labels, shadow outlines, things used together stored together."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'Seiri (Sorting), Seiso (Shining) and Shitsuke (Sustaining) all start with "S" in Japanese too, which is exactly why the exam uses them as distractors.'}],final:"Seiton = Straightening → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Sorting is Seiri, the first S.","","Shining is Seiso, the third S.","Sustaining is Shitsuke, the fifth S."],trap:"The book advises learning the English words — but the exam may still show the Japanese one.",bookRef:"Book p. 503"},{id:"sv-5-202",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"During **Straightening** in a regional field-maintenance workshop, the team stores every item by how often it is used. The fibre **OTDR test set** is used about **once a month**. Where should it be kept?",options:['Within arm’s reach on the main workbench (an "A" item)','On a nearby shelf or cabinet next to the bench (a "B" item)','In a remote storage location a few minutes away (a "C" item)',"Nowhere — anything not used daily should be removed during Sort"],answer:2,hint:"ABC storage: A = daily → arm’s reach; B = weekly → nearby; C = monthly or quarterly → remote location.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s ABC storage rule ties distance to frequency of use: **A** (daily) within arm’s reach, **B** (weekly) nearby but off the work surface, **C** (monthly or quarterly) in a remote location a few minutes away."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Once a month is a **C** item, so the OTDR goes to remote storage, freeing the bench for daily items."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Arm’s reach and nearby shelves are for A and B items. Sort removes only items that are *not needed*; a monthly-use test set is still needed."}],final:"Monthly use → C item → remote storage → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Arm’s reach is reserved for A items used daily or several times a day.","A nearby shelf is for B items used about weekly.","",'Confuses "not used daily" with "not needed"; Sort removes useless items, not occasional ones.'],bookRef:"Book pp. 504–505"},{id:"sv-5-203",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"In a shared test lab used by three engineering teams, the benches near the door are always dusty and covered in old cable offcuts. Each team says that area belongs to someone else. Which **Shining** practice from the book addresses this BEST?",options:["Hire an outside contractor for a quarterly deep clean of the whole lab","Red-tag the benches near the door and move them to a holding area","Use a layout map that assigns every area, including the shared benches, to a named person or team for cleaning","Add the benches to the monthly Sustain audit and post the score"],answer:2,hint:'Shining: clean, prevent mess, and remove any "no man’s land" by giving every area an owner.',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book’s Shining step uses a **layout map** to give each person or team a cleaning area, so no area is "no man’s land". It also stresses mess prevention.'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The problem is exactly a no-man’s-land: nobody owns the door benches. Assigning them on the map fixes ownership of cleaning."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A one-off contractor clean does not build the daily habit; red-tagging is Sort; an audit measures the problem but does not assign who cleans."}],final:"Assign cleaning areas on a layout map → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A periodic outside clean is a one-off event; nobody in the lab owns the area afterwards.","Red-tagging belongs to Sort and is for unneeded items, not dirty benches that are still needed.","","An audit (Sustain) would record the dirt but still leaves the area without an owner."],bookRef:"Book p. 505"},{id:"sv-5-204",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"The book splits the five S’s into **workplace actions** and **sustaining actions**. Which two S’s are the **sustaining** actions?",options:["Sorting and Straightening","Straightening and Shining","Standardizing and Sustaining","Shining and Sustaining"],answer:2,hint:"First three S’s = workplace actions; last two S’s = sustaining actions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Sorting, Straightening and Shining change the workplace. **Standardizing and Sustaining** hold those changes in place — that is why 5S counts as a Control method."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The sustaining pair is the 4th and 5th S: Standardizing (rules, visual controls) and Sustaining (discipline, schedules, audits)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Any pair that includes Sort, Straighten or Shine contains a workplace action."}],final:"Sustaining actions = S4 + S5 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["These are the first two workplace actions.","Both are workplace actions (S2 and S3).","","Mixes a workplace action (Shining) with a sustaining action."],bookRef:"Book p. 503"},{id:"sv-5-205",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"The book lists signs that a project area needs **5S as a control**. All of the following are such signs EXCEPT:",options:["Outliers keep appearing in the project metric","The control chart shows only common-cause variation and Cpk has stayed well above 1.33 since handoff","The initial gains from the project are being lost","The process capability that was improved is now dropping"],answer:1,hint:"Signs that 5S is needed: outliers, loss of initial gains, declining benefits or capability.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says to 5S the project area when you see **outliers**, **loss of initial gains**, or **declining benefits / dropping capability** — waste that differs between people or shifts shows up this way."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A stable chart with Cpk well above 1.33 that has *stayed* there shows the gains are holding, so it is not a warning sign."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other three are word-for-word the book’s warning signs."}],final:"Stable and capable is not a warning sign → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Outliers in the metric are one of the book’s listed signs.","","Loss of initial gains is one of the book’s listed signs.","Declining capability is one of the book’s listed signs."],bookRef:"Book pp. 512–513"},{id:"sv-5-206",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"The book recommends launching 5S in every department at the same time, because starting in a pilot area slows the roll-out. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"5S starts in a pilot area and then spreads step by step.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book recommends starting 5S in a **pilot area** and spreading it step by step; its strength depends on the commitment of the people who maintain it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A big-bang launch everywhere is the opposite of that advice, so the statement is false."}],final:"Start with a pilot area → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The book advises a pilot area first, then a step-by-step spread — not a simultaneous launch.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 503"},{id:"sv-5-207",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`The weekly 5S audit report for three areas is shown (each S scored 0–100, target 80%):

\`\`\`
5S Audit Summary – Week 42                       Target = 80%
Area            Sort  Straighten  Shine  Standardize  Sustain   Total
Store room A     90       85        80        80         75       82%
NOC floor        95       90        85        55         50       75%
Training lab     70       65        75        80         85       75%
\`\`\`

Which area shows the typical pattern of a 5S programme that is **not being held in place**, and what should it focus on?`,options:["Training lab — Sort is the lowest score, so it should start again with a red-tag Sort","NOC floor — the workplace S’s are strong but Standardize and Sustain are weak, so it needs standards, visual controls and a regular audit routine","Store room A — it has the lowest Sustain score of the areas above target, so it needs a new clean-up event","NOC floor and Training lab equally — both total 75%, so both need the same action"],answer:1,hint:"First three S’s = workplace actions; last two = sustaining actions. Read the pattern, not only the total.",steps:[{title:"Read the totals",body:"$$\\text{NOC}=\\dfrac{95+90+85+55+50}{5}=\\dfrac{375}{5}=75\\%$$ The Training lab is also 75%, Store room A is 82%."},{title:"Read the pattern",body:"NOC floor: Sort, Straighten and Shine are 85–95, but **Standardize 55 and Sustain 50**. The workplace was organised, but nothing keeps it that way — the sustaining actions are failing."},{title:"Decide",body:"The cure for weak S4/S5 is standards, visual controls, checklists, audits and discipline. The Training lab shows the opposite pattern (weak workplace S’s, strong sustaining S’s), so it needs a different action."}],final:"NOC floor: S4 and S5 weak → strengthen standards and audits → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The Training lab’s pattern is the reverse (weak workplace S’s); and it is not the area failing to hold its gains.","","Store room A is above target; another clean-up event is a workplace action and does not fix sustaining.","Equal totals hide opposite patterns that need different actions."],trap:"The same total can hide very different problems — read which S’s are low.",bookRef:"Book pp. 503–505"},{id:"sv-5-208",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A provisioning team has sorted its shared drive, given every order type one fixed folder, and removed duplicate files (the first three S’s are done). Which action belongs to the **fourth S**?",options:["Delete every file that has not been opened in the last year","Run a monthly audit of the drive and post the score on the team board","Move the most-used templates to the top-level folder","Agree a written naming convention and folder template, with colour-coded labels, that everyone uses"],answer:3,hint:"Standardizing = the rules and visual controls that keep the first three S’s in place.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The fourth S, **Standardizing**, keeps the workplace at the level reached by Sort, Straighten and Shine through standards and visual controls (labels, colour-coding, standard methods)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A shared naming convention and folder template with colour-coded labels is exactly that kind of standard."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Deleting unused files is Sort; placing the most-used items closest is Straighten; regular audits are the discipline of Sustain."}],final:"Fourth S = Standardize → naming standard + visual labels → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Removing unneeded items is Sort, the first S.","Audits and schedules are tools of Sustaining, the fifth S.","Placing items by frequency of use is Straightening, the second S.",""],trap:"Do not confuse Standardize (the rules) with Sustain (the discipline of following and auditing them).",bookRef:"Book pp. 504–505"},{id:"sv-5-209",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`The book shows Lean controls as a pyramid that is built **from the bottom up**. Put these levels in order, from the base to the top:

- **P** — Kaizen
- **Q** — 5S workplace organisation
- **R** — Standardized Work
- **S** — Kanban
- **T** — Visual Factory`,options:["Q → R → T → P → S","T → Q → R → S → P","Q → T → P → R → S","Q → T → R → P → S"],answer:3,hint:"Each level cannot be sustained without the one below it: 5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The pyramid is **5S → Visual Factory → Standardized Work → Kaizen → Kanban**. Each level needs the one beneath it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Q (5S) → T (Visual Factory) → R (Standardized Work) → P (Kaizen) → S (Kanban)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Kanban is always the top, 5S always the base; the usual trap is swapping Visual Factory and Standardized Work, or Kaizen and Standardized Work."}],final:"Q → T → R → P → S → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Swaps Visual Factory and Standardized Work; standards need visual controls first.","Puts Visual Factory below 5S and Kanban below Kaizen — 5S is the base and Kanban the top.","Puts Kaizen below Standardized Work; Kaizen needs standard work to improve on.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 502"},{id:"sv-5-210",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A NOC team applies 5S to its monitoring dashboard of 60 widgets: **20** are used every shift, **15** are exact duplicates of other widgets, and **25** nobody can say whether they are needed. Which treatment follows the book’s Sorting rules?",options:["Delete all 40 widgets that are not used every shift immediately","Keep all 60 widgets but move the 40 into a hidden tab in case they are needed","Hold all 40 non-daily widgets for a set period before deciding anything","Keep the 20, delete the 15 duplicates now, and hold the 25 unknown widgets for a set period, removing them if nobody uses them"],answer:3,hint:"Sort into useful (keep), useless (dispose now) and unknown (hold for a set time, then remove if unused).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book sorts everything into three groups: **useful** — keep; **useless** — dispose of immediately; **unknown** — leave for a set period, and remove if nobody uses it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Used every shift = useful. Exact duplicates = useless, so delete now. Unknown = hold for a set time, then decide."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Deleting the unknown items at once skips the holding period; hiding everything keeps the clutter; holding the duplicates delays a decision that is already clear."}],final:"Useful keep · useless delete · unknown hold → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Treats the unknown group like the useless group; unknown items get a holding period first.","Hiding items is not sorting — the clutter stays and nobody decides.","The duplicates are clearly useless and should be removed now, not held.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 504"},{id:"sv-5-211",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A NOC store manager wants a control that lets anyone see **at a glance** when a test set has not been returned to its place. Which option is the BEST visual control for this?",options:["A procedure in the document-management system describing where items belong","An annual refresher training session on store-room rules","A shadow board with painted outlines and colour-coded labels for every test set","A monthly e-mail reminding staff to put equipment back"],answer:2,hint:"Visual controls (outlines, painted lines, labels, colour-coding) make an abnormal condition obvious at the point of use.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Standardizing is maintained by **visual controls** — signboards, painted lines, shadow outlines, colour-coding — so that a missing or misplaced item is seen immediately."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"An empty outline on a shadow board shows a missing test set instantly, with no reading required."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A procedure in a system, a training session and an e-mail all rely on memory and are not visible where the work happens."}],final:"Shadow board = visual control → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A stored procedure is not visible at the shelf; nobody sees a gap by reading it.","Training is periodic and relies on memory, not on seeing the abnormal condition.","","A reminder e-mail is a soft request, not a visual signal at the point of use."],bookRef:"Book pp. 504–505"},{id:"sv-5-212",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A field-service depot issues **48 ONT power adaptors per day**. Replenishment takes **1.5 days**, the safety factor is **20%**, and each bin holds **12 adaptors**. How many kanban cards are needed?",options:["7","8","6","96"],answer:1,hint:"$$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$ and always round up.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"D = 48 per day, L = 1.5 days, S = 0.20, C = 12 per bin."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$N=\\dfrac{48\\times 1.5\\times 1.2}{12}=\\dfrac{86.4}{12}=7.2$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"A fraction of a card does not exist, so **round up** to 8 cards."}],final:"N = 7.2 → 8 cards → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Rounded 7.2 down; a loop with 7 cards cannot cover the safety allowance.","","Left out the safety factor: 48 × 1.5 ÷ 12 = 6.","Gave the maximum number of adaptors in the loop (8 × 12), not the number of cards."],trap:"Round kanban numbers UP, never to the nearest whole number.",bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-213",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A warehouse manager wants to introduce kanban in a spares store where parts have no fixed locations, labels are missing, and each storekeeper reorders in his own way. According to the book, what should come FIRST?",options:["Calculate the number of cards and launch kanban now; the cards will force discipline","Buy an electronic kanban system with barcode scanners so no labels are needed","Put the lower levels in place — 5S, visual controls and standardized replenishment work — because kanban started too early will backfire","Launch kanban with a 50% safety factor to absorb the disorder"],answer:2,hint:"Kanban sits at the top of the Lean pyramid; without 5S, Visual Factory, Standardized Work and Kaizen it cannot succeed.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book is direct: without 5S, Visual Factory, Standardized Work and ongoing Kaizen, **kanbans cannot succeed**, and kanban introduced too early **will backfire**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"No fixed locations (no 5S/Straightening), no labels (no visual controls), everyone reorders differently (no standard work) — the foundations are missing."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Cards, software or a big safety factor all sit on top of the same chaos; a large safety factor just adds inventory."}],final:"Build the foundations before kanban → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The book warns that premature kanban backfires; cards do not create discipline on their own.","Technology does not replace locations, labels and standard work.","","A large safety factor only hides the disorder with extra inventory."],bookRef:"Book pp. 502, 512"},{id:"sv-5-214",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"At the end of a router-refurbishment line, a card is posted that tells the line to **start the next batch** when finished stock runs down. In the book’s classification, which kanban is this?",options:["A Signal Kanban — a finished-goods (intra-process) kanban","A Withdrawal Kanban — an incoming-material (inter-process) kanban","A P.I.K. used to pull parts from the supplier","A supplier kanban that authorises a purchase order"],answer:0,hint:"Finished-goods (intra-process): Signal Kanban, P.I.K. Incoming-material (inter-process): Withdrawal / supplier.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Finished-goods kanbans control production **inside** a process. The **Signal Kanban** is posted at the end of the processing area to **signal production to begin**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The card is at the end of the line and tells the line itself to make more — a Signal Kanban."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Withdrawal and supplier kanbans fetch material **from** another department or supplier; a P.I.K. is a finished-goods kanban too, but it is not used with suppliers."}],final:"Start-production signal at end of line = Signal Kanban → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Withdrawal kanbans pull material from a supplying process; nothing is withdrawn here.","A P.I.K. belongs to finished goods, but it does not pull from suppliers.","Supplier kanbans request incoming material, not production in this line."],trap:"Memory aid: finished goods → MAKE something; incoming material → WITHDRAW something.",bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-215",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What is a **P.I.K.** (Production Instruction Kanban) used for, according to the book?",options:["Finer inventory control: it is posted as inventory is used up, so only the minimum stock level is kept","Pulling material from a supplying department between two processes","Authorising overtime when customer demand peaks","Recording defects found in production for the SPC chart"],answer:0,hint:"P.I.K. is a finished-goods kanban posted as inventory is depleted, keeping only the minimum level.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists two finished-goods kanbans: the Signal Kanban and the **P.I.K.**, which gives **finer inventory control** — it is posted as inventory is depleted so only the minimum level is kept."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Pulling from a supplying department is a Withdrawal (incoming-material) kanban; overtime and defect logging are not kanban functions at all."}],final:"P.I.K. = minimum-stock production signal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","That describes a Withdrawal (incoming-material) kanban.","Kanban limits production to real usage; it is not an overtime authorisation.","Defect recording is data collection for SPC, not a production instruction."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-216",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"The book describes kanban as a quick fix for large inventory problems and for strongly fluctuating demand. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Kanban is not a quick fix; it needs stable demand and the lower pyramid tools.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book states kanban is **not a quick fix** for large inventory problems, workforce issues, poor planning or fluctuating demand, and quotes up to seven years to run it through a full supply chain."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The statement claims the opposite, so it is false."}],final:"Kanban is not a quick fix → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The book explicitly says kanban is not a quick fix for inventory or fluctuating demand.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-217",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A store manager wants kanban cards to end stock-outs of 5G home routers. The demand report shows:

\`\`\`
Weekly demand – 5G CPE units (Store 14)
Week     31    32    33    34    35    36
Units   120   310    85   270    60   295
Mean = 190      StDev = 113.7
\`\`\`

What is the BEST response, based on the book?`,options:["Demand swings far too much (the standard deviation is over half the mean); kanban needs a relatively stable demand cycle, so first work on levelling or planning demand","Size the loop on the mean of 190 per week with a 10% safety factor and launch it","Size the loop on the peak week of 310 so that no stock-out can happen","Kanban is ideal here because a pull system reacts automatically to any change in demand"],answer:0,hint:"Kanban needs relatively stable demand; it is not a fix for fluctuating demand.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Weekly demand jumps between 60 and 310; the standard deviation (113.7) is more than half of the mean (190)."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"The book lists a **relatively stable demand cycle** as a condition for kanban and says it is not a quick fix for fluctuating demand."},{title:"Decide",body:"Stabilise or plan demand (for example promotions, ordering patterns) before sizing a kanban loop."}],final:"Unstable demand → stabilise before kanban → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A loop sized on the mean with 10% safety will run out in every peak week of this pattern.","Sizing on the peak builds permanent excess inventory — the waste kanban is meant to remove.","Kanban follows usage only within the stock its cards allow; large swings break the loop."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-218",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Black Belt audits a kanban loop for SFP optical modules:

\`\`\`
Kanban Loop Audit – SFP modules (depot 3)
Cards calculated by formula          7
Cards in circulation                12
Average full bins on shelf           8.5
Stock-outs last quarter              0
Oldest full bin on shelf            41 days
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["The loop is working perfectly — zero stock-outs proves the number of cards is right","The loop has 5 cards too many: extra bins just sit on the shelf (one for 41 days), so cards should be removed towards the calculated number while watching for stock-outs","More cards are needed, because a bin waiting 41 days shows that replenishment is too slow","The loop should be replaced by forecast-based replenishment because kanban is causing excess stock"],answer:1,hint:"The number of cards caps the inventory in the loop; extra cards mean extra stock.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"The formula needs 7 cards but 12 circulate; on average 8.5 full bins sit on the shelf and the oldest has waited 41 days."},{title:"Apply the idea",body:"Each card authorises one container of stock. Five extra cards = five extra bins of inventory that hide problems instead of exposing them."},{title:"Decide",body:"Pull cards out of the loop step by step towards 7 and watch for stock-outs — that is how kanban drives inventory down."}],final:"Too many cards → excess stock → remove cards → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Zero stock-outs is expected when there is far too much stock; it does not prove the loop is right.","","A bin that waits 41 days means stock is not being used, not that replenishment is slow.","The excess comes from too many cards, not from the pull principle; going back to push would add more stock."],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-219",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In its purest form, what does the book say moves in a kanban facility **without** a kanban attached?",options:["Urgent customer orders","Raw material arriving from suppliers","Nothing","Finished goods going into stock"],answer:2,hint:"In pure kanban, nothing moves without a kanban signal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says that in its purest form **nothing moves** in the facility without a kanban attached — production, conveyance and delivery all follow the signal."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Each of the other options describes material moving without a pull signal, which is push behaviour."}],final:"Nothing moves without a kanban → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Even urgent orders move with a signal in a pure kanban system.","Incoming material is pulled with incoming-material (withdrawal) kanbans.","","Moving goods into stock without a signal is overproduction — push."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-220",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A depot sends a card to the regional warehouse each time a bin of fibre splitters is emptied; the warehouse then sends back one full bin. In the book’s terms, which kanban is this?",options:["An incoming-material (Withdrawal, inter-process) kanban","A Signal Kanban (finished goods)","A Production Instruction Kanban (P.I.K.)","A push replenishment order based on forecast"],answer:0,hint:"Incoming-material kanban = Withdrawal / inter-process / supplier: gets material from a supplying department.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s second category, **incoming-material kanban** (also called withdrawal, inter-process or supplier kanban), is used to get material from a supplying department and regulates WIP between two processes."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The depot withdraws splitters from the warehouse, a separate supplying process, one bin per card."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Signal Kanban and P.I.K. tell a process to *produce*; a forecast-based order is push, not a pull signal."}],final:"Withdraw from a supplying process = Withdrawal kanban → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A Signal Kanban tells a process to start production; the depot produces nothing here.","A P.I.K. is a production signal for minimum stock, not a withdrawal from another site.","The order is triggered by actual usage (an empty bin), not by a forecast."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-221",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'In a kanban loop for patch cords, storekeepers fill each returning bin with "whatever fits" — sometimes 20 cords, sometimes 45. Stock levels swing and nobody can say how many cards the loop needs. What is missing?',options:["A standard, small quantity of parts per card","A higher safety factor","A forecast-based push schedule","More kanban cards in the loop"],answer:0,hint:"Kanban conditions include a standard number of parts per card, kept as small as possible.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists a **standard number of parts per card, as small as possible**, among the conditions for a working kanban."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"If the quantity per card changes every time, each card represents a different amount of stock and the loop cannot be sized or controlled."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"More safety stock or more cards add inventory without fixing the root problem; a push schedule abandons pull."}],final:"Fix the quantity per card → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A higher safety factor adds stock but the per-card quantity still varies.","Going back to push removes the pull signal instead of fixing it.","More cards add inventory; the loop is still uncontrolled while quantities vary."],bookRef:"Book p. 512"},{id:"sv-5-222",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`Put the steps of one cycle in a two-bin kanban loop for wall clips in order:

- **P** — The full bin, with its card, is returned to the point of use
- **Q** — The bin at the point of use is emptied
- **R** — The supplier fills a bin with exactly the card quantity
- **S** — The card is sent to the supplier`,options:["Q → R → S → P","S → Q → R → P","Q → S → P → R","Q → S → R → P"],answer:3,hint:"Pull: consumption creates the signal, the signal triggers replenishment of the card quantity, the full container returns.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In pull, **use triggers the signal**. Nothing is made or moved until the card arrives."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Bin emptied (Q) → card sent (S) → supplier fills the card quantity (R) → full bin and card back at the point of use (P)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Filling before the card arrives is push; sending the card before the bin is empty creates stock nobody used."}],final:"Q → S → R → P → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The supplier refills before receiving the signal — that is push.","The card is sent before the bin is empty, so replenishment is not triggered by use.","The full bin is returned before it has been filled.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-223",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"In a pull system, all of the following can act as a kanban signal EXCEPT:",options:["A weekly production schedule based on the sales forecast","A small card attached to a container","An empty container returned to the supplying process","An empty, marked square on the floor where a full tote should stand"],answer:0,hint:"A kanban is a signal of actual use: a card or a physical signal such as an empty container.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines kanban as a communication **signal or card** — a small card **or a physical signal such as an empty container**. The signal comes from actual use."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A card, an empty container and an empty marked space all show that something was used. A forecast-based schedule tells a process to make things whether or not they were used — that is push."}],final:"Forecast schedule = push, not a kanban → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A card is the classic kanban signal.","The book names an empty container as a kanban signal.","An empty marked space is a physical signal of use, the same idea as an empty container."],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-224",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A SIM tray in a handset accepts the SIM card only when its **cut corner** is in the correct position; a SIM inserted the wrong way simply does not fit. Which poka-yoke method does this illustrate?",options:["Fixed-value method — a set number of parts must be used","Contact method — a physical shape makes the wrong orientation impossible","Motion-step method — the steps must be done in the right order","Warning method — a signal alerts the user after the error"],answer:1,hint:"Contact = shape, size or a sensor checks a physical feature; fixed-value = a set count; motion-step = a set order.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Poka-yoke methods (general Lean practice): **contact** (shape, size or sensor checks a physical feature), **fixed-value** (a set number of actions or parts), **motion-step** (steps in the right order)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The cut corner is a physical feature that only fits one way — a contact method, and a prevention device: the wrong insertion cannot happen."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"No counting is involved (fixed-value), no sequence (motion-step), and no alert is given — the error is simply blocked."}],final:"Shape that fits one way = contact method → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Fixed-value counts parts or actions; nothing is counted here.","","Motion-step enforces an order of steps; there is only one step here.","Nothing warns the user — the design makes the error impossible, which is stronger than a warning."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-225",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Installers receive a kit tray with exactly **six** labelled slots (ONT, power adaptor, patch cord, two wall clips, quick guide). The job app will not close the order until the installer confirms the tray is empty. Which poka-yoke method is this?",options:["Fixed-value method","Contact method","Motion-step method","Type 3 inspection (containment)"],answer:0,hint:"Fixed-value = a set number of parts or actions must be completed before moving on.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **fixed-value** method checks that a set number of parts or actions has been completed — the classic example is a kit tray with a fixed number of slots."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Six slots must all be empty before the job can close, so a missing item is obvious and blocks closure."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"No shape or sensor checks a feature (contact); no particular order of steps is enforced (motion-step); and the check is built into the step, not a later inspection."}],final:"Fixed count of parts = fixed-value → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Contact checks a physical feature such as shape or size, not a count.","Motion-step enforces the order of steps, not the number of parts.","Inspection finds defects after the work; this device stops the job at the source."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-226",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Two designs are proposed for a billing-system check on an invalid tax code. Design X shows a yellow warning banner but lets the agent continue. Design Y stops the invoice from being issued until the code is corrected. Which statement is correct?",options:["Design Y is stronger, because a control (shut-down) response stops the process until the error is fixed","Design X is stronger, because agents learn more from warnings","Both are equally strong, because both detect the error at the source","Neither is a poka-yoke, because neither is a physical device"],answer:0,hint:"Control (shut-down) response > warning response, because people can ignore a warning.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A poka-yoke can respond with a **control (shut-down)** — the process stops — or a **warning** — a light, sound or banner. A warning is weaker because a person can ignore it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Design Y blocks the invoice: the defect cannot move forward. Design X relies on the agent reading and acting on the banner."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Detecting the error is not enough if the defect can still pass; and poka-yoke does not need to be physical — software rules count."}],final:"Shut-down beats warning → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Warnings can be clicked past; learning is not the goal of a poka-yoke.","Both detect, but only Y stops the defect from moving forward.","Software rules are valid mistake-proofing; a physical device is not required."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-227",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"In the book’s ranking of control methods, what is a **Type 3 corrective action**?",options:["A countermeasure that removes the error condition so the defect is never created","A flag that detects the error condition and shuts the process down","Inspection — a containment action, such as audits or 100% inspection, that finds defects after they are made","SPC on the critical X’s with trained, empowered staff"],answer:2,hint:"Type 1 = countermeasure (prevent); Type 2 = flag (detect and stop); Type 3 = inspection (contain).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s three corrective-action types: **Type 1** countermeasure (the defect is never created), **Type 2** flag (detect and shut down), **Type 3** **inspection** — a short-term containment action that finds defects."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:'The countermeasure is Type 1, the flag is Type 2, and SPC with empowered staff is ranked between Type 2 and Type 3 but is not itself a "type".'}],final:"Type 3 = inspection (containment) → answer **C**",whyWrong:["That is Type 1, the countermeasure.","That is Type 2, the flag.","","SPC with empowered staff is ranked third in the list, but it is not a Type 3 corrective action."],trap:'Do not confuse "ranked third" with "Type 3".',bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-228",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"According to the book’s ranking of control methods, which of these controls is the LEAST effective?",options:["A Type 2 flag that detects the error and shuts the step down","SPC on the critical X’s with trained and empowered staff","A standard operating procedure (SOP) on its own","100% inspection of the output (Type 3)"],answer:2,hint:"Ranking: Type 1 countermeasure > Type 2 flag > SPC with empowered staff > Type 3 inspection > SOP alone.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book ranks: (1) Type 1 countermeasure, (2) Type 2 flag, (3) SPC on X’s with empowered staff, (4) Type 3 inspection, (5) **SOP alone** — not sustainable."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Of the four options, the SOP on its own is ranked last."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Many people pick 100% inspection as weakest, but the book places it above an SOP alone because inspection at least finds defects."}],final:"SOP alone ranks lowest → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The Type 2 flag is ranked second — strong, because the defect cannot move on.","SPC with empowered staff is ranked third.","","Inspection is ranked fourth: weak, but above an SOP that nobody checks."],trap:"Trap: 100% inspection feels weakest, but an SOP alone is lower in the book’s list.",bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-229",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`An extract from a draft control plan for fibre activation is shown:

\`\`\`
Control Plan – Fibre activation (extract)
Step  Control                                            Type recorded
1     Provisioning system assigns the VLAN from the port   Type 1 – countermeasure
2     Config tool blocks commit if the syntax check fails   Type 2 – flag
3     Supervisor re-checks 10% of orders every Friday       Type 1 – countermeasure
4     SOP v3 issued; read-and-sign completed                Procedure
\`\`\`

Which entry is **misclassified**?`,options:["Step 1 — automatic assignment only flags wrong VLANs, so it is Type 2","Step 2 — blocking the commit means the error is never made, so it is Type 1","Step 4 — a read-and-sign record turns the SOP into a poka-yoke","Step 3 — a sample re-check finds errors after they are made, so it is Type 3 inspection, not a countermeasure"],answer:3,hint:"Type 1 removes the error condition; Type 2 detects and stops; Type 3 inspects after the fact.",steps:[{title:"Read each row",body:"Step 1: the VLAN is assigned automatically, so a wrong manual entry cannot happen → Type 1 ✔. Step 2: the bad configuration exists but is detected and the commit is stopped → Type 2 ✔."},{title:"Find the error",body:"Step 3: a weekly 10% re-check finds errors **after** they are made and only in a sample → **Type 3 inspection**, not a countermeasure."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Step 4 is correctly recorded as a procedure — signatures do not make an SOP mistake-proof."}],final:"Step 3 is inspection (Type 3) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Automatic assignment removes the manual error condition — that is a countermeasure (Type 1).","The syntax error is still made; the tool detects it and stops the step — a flag (Type 2).","Read-and-sign proves people received the SOP; it does not stop an error.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-230",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"A detection-type poka-yoke does not stop the error from occurring, but it stops the resulting defect from moving on to the next step. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Type 2 (flag) = detect the error condition and shut down so the defect does not move forward.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s **Type 2** corrective action (flag) detects that the error condition has occurred and shuts the process down so the defect does not move forward."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That is exactly the statement: the error happens, but it is caught at once and stopped — so it is true."}],final:"Detection stops the defect moving on → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Detection poka-yoke really does let the error occur — preventing it altogether is the job of a Type 1 countermeasure."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-231",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A team has budget for **two** mistake-proofing devices in the order-to-activation process. How should it decide where to put them?",options:["Use the FMEA to target the failure modes with the highest risk (RPN), preferring designs that remove the error condition","Put both devices at the final inspection step, where all errors can be seen","Choose the steps where staff complain the most","Choose the steps where the cheapest devices are available"],answer:0,hint:"Use FMEA risk (RPN) to choose targets; prefer Type 1 prevention, then Type 2 detection.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Poka-yoke effort goes where the **risk** is highest. The FMEA ranks failure modes by RPN, and the control ranking says to prefer removing the error condition (Type 1) over detecting it (Type 2)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"With only two devices, pick the two highest-risk failure modes and design them out if possible."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A device at final inspection is containment, not mistake-proofing at the source; complaints and price are not risk measures."}],final:"Highest-RPN failure modes first → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Placing devices at final inspection only contains defects after they are made.","Complaints may not match the risk to the customer.","Cost matters, but choosing on price alone ignores where the risk is."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-232",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book’s **Improvement Selection Matrix**, which kind of improvement receives the **highest** impact score?",options:["One where personnel are trained not to make the error","One where the output is inspected 100%","One where the X’s are removed from impacting the output","One where a new written procedure is issued"],answer:2,hint:"Remove root cause > monitor/flag > inspect > train people (impact 7 down to 1).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The matrix gives impact **7** when the X’s are **removed** from impacting the output, and only **1** when personnel are trained. Removing the root cause outranks monitoring, which outranks inspection, which outranks training."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Training, inspection and procedures all leave the error condition in place."}],final:"X removed from impacting the output = top score → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Training gets the lowest impact score (1) in the matrix.","Inspection finds defects after they occur and ranks below removal and flagging.","","A procedure alone is one of the weakest controls."],bookRef:"Book pp. 557, 560"},{id:"sv-5-233",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A new rule blocks a technician from closing a fault ticket without a valid root-cause code. Before the control plan lists the rule as an effective control, what should the team do?",options:["Assume it works because the IT team has signed it off","Wait a year and check whether ticket-coding errors have fallen","Send a memo telling technicians that the rule now exists","Challenge-test it: deliberately try to close tickets with missing or invalid codes, confirm the block works, and record the test and an owner in the control plan"],answer:3,hint:"Verify a poka-yoke works (test it on purpose) and record it in the control plan.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A mistake-proofing device must be **verified** — test it on purpose with a known error — and then recorded in the control plan with an owner, so its failure is noticed."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Try to close tickets with no code or an invalid code; if the block fires every time, document it."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Sign-off is not evidence; waiting a year lets defects through if it is broken; a memo is communication, not verification."}],final:"Test the device deliberately, then document it → answer **D**",whyWrong:["IT sign-off does not prove that the block fires on real error conditions.","A year of waiting means any failure goes unnoticed for months.","A memo informs people but does not prove the rule works.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-234",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"An agent forgot to apply a goodwill credit that a supervisor had already approved. Management proposes a written warning and retraining. Which response reflects the **poka-yoke** mindset?",options:["Treat forgetting as a normal human error and change the process so that an approved credit is applied automatically by the system","Retrain all agents and add a quiz on the credit procedure","Add a monthly audit of 5% of approved credits","Put up posters reminding agents to apply credits"],answer:0,hint:"Poka-yoke designs the error out of the process instead of relying on people remembering.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Poka-yoke assumes people **will** forget, mix up and misread, and designs the process so the error cannot happen or is caught at once."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"If approval automatically triggers the credit, there is no step left to forget — a Type 1 countermeasure."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Training and posters rely on memory (lowest in the ranking); a 5% audit is inspection that finds some errors after customers are affected."}],final:"Design the forgetting out → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Training ranks lowest in the book’s matrix; it leaves the error condition in place.","A small audit is containment and misses most errors.","Posters rely on memory and attention, the very things that failed."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 557"},{id:"sv-5-235",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A network-design team completes only about **three change designs per week**, and each design takes several days. The team wants to chart **design cycle time** (days). Which chart is MOST appropriate?",options:["I-MR chart","Xbar-R chart with subgroups of 5 designs","Xbar-S chart","p chart"],answer:0,hint:"Continuous data with low volume or long cycle times → subgroup size 1 → I-MR.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The selection tree starts with the data type, then subgroup size. Low production volume, long cycle times or one value per period → **subgroup size 1 → I-MR**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Cycle time is continuous, and three designs a week cannot form timely rational subgroups — waiting for 5 would mix almost two weeks of conditions."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Xbar-R and Xbar-S need subgroups; a p chart is for defectives (yes or no) and throws away the cycle-time information."}],final:"Low volume, continuous → I-MR → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Five designs would take almost two weeks to collect, so the subgroup would not be rational.","Xbar-S is for large subgroups (10 or more).","A p chart needs pass or fail data; cycle time is continuous."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-236",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A project cut fibre-repair time. In Control, the team plans to chart only the **monthly customer-satisfaction score** (the Y). According to the book, what should the team consider FIRST?",options:["Chart only the Y, because that is what the customer experiences","Draw the satisfaction target on the chart as the lower control limit","Stop charting, because the improvement has already been proven","Put SPC on the vital few X’s found in Analyze (for example parts availability and dispatch delay), because the Y is controlled by controlling the X’s"],answer:3,hint:"Y = f(X): charting X’s = SPC (control); charting the Y = SPM (monitoring).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book reminds you of $Y=f(X)$: an output is controlled by controlling its inputs. Charting the inputs is **SPC**; charting the output is **SPM** (monitoring). When the vital X’s are known, **first consider SPC on the X’s**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A monthly satisfaction score only shows a problem long after it happens. Charts on the critical X’s warn before the Y moves."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Targets are not control limits, and stopping measurement is how gains are lost."}],final:"SPC on the X’s first → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Charting only the Y is monitoring; it reacts after the customer is affected.","Control limits come from process data, never from a target.","Control charts are exactly what sustains the improvement after the project.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 516–517"},{id:"sv-5-237",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A contact-centre KPI goes out of control about **3 times a month** (take a month as **4 weeks**). Using the book’s rule of thumb for sampling frequency, about how many subgroups should be taken **per week**, at least?",options:["30","1","8","3"],answer:2,hint:"Sample at least 10 times more often than out-of-control conditions occur.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Out-of-control events: 3 per month; 1 month = 4 weeks; rule of thumb: sample at least 10 times more often."},{title:"Pick the rule",body:"Sampling rate ≥ 10 × the event rate, expressed in the same time unit (per week)."},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\text{subgroups per week}=\\dfrac{3\\times 10}{4}=\\dfrac{30}{4}=7.5$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Round up: at least **8 subgroups per week** (more than one per working day)."}],final:"At least 8 subgroups per week → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is the monthly number (3 × 10); it was not converted to a weekly rate.","Converted 3 per month to 0.75 per week but forgot to multiply by 10.","","Sampled at the same rate as the events occur — far too rare to catch a shift quickly."],trap:"Put the events and the samples in the same time unit before comparing.",bookRef:"Book pp. 528–529"},{id:"sv-5-238",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Activation centre opening hours are **07:00–23:00**. The data collection log for a new Xbar-R chart of activation time reads:

\`\`\`
Data collection log – Activation time (min), subgroup n = 4
Date      Sample time     Collected by
02-Oct    08:05–08:20     Night-shift handover clerk
03-Oct    08:02–08:18     Night-shift handover clerk
04-Oct    08:10–08:25     Night-shift handover clerk
...       (all 25 subgroups taken between 08:00 and 08:30)
\`\`\`

What is the main problem with these data?`,options:["The sample is biased: only the half-hour after shift handover is sampled, so the chart does not represent the process across the day","A subgroup size of 4 is too small for an Xbar-R chart","25 subgroups are too few to set trial control limits","Consecutive activations cannot form a rational subgroup"],answer:0,hint:"Samples must represent the population while the process runs normally; biased samples make the chart invalid.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Every subgroup comes from 08:00–08:30, straight after the handover, although the centre runs 07:00–23:00."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"The book warns against biased samples (for example only the first shift or only just before lunch). The sample must represent the whole population of activations; otherwise the chart is **invalid**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"n = 4 is a normal Xbar-R subgroup, 25 subgroups is the usual minimum, and consecutive units are the textbook rational subgroup."}],final:"All samples from one half-hour → biased → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Subgroups of 2–5 are exactly what Xbar-R is designed for.","Twenty to twenty-five subgroups is the usual basis for trial limits.","Consecutive units made under the same conditions are the standard rational subgroup."],bookRef:"Book pp. 517–519"},{id:"sv-5-239",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`The same **125** consecutive provisioning times were charted two ways:

\`\`\`
Chart 1: I-MR (each value plotted)
  I Chart test results:     No test failures.
Chart 2: Xbar-R (25 subgroups of 5 consecutive orders)
  Xbar Chart: TEST 1 failed at subgroups 18, 19
  R Chart:    No test failures.
\`\`\`

Why does only the Xbar chart signal?`,options:["The I-MR chart is wrong, because individual values must never be charted","The Xbar chart signals falsely, because its limits come from the specification","The R chart controls the Xbar limits, so any R value raises an Xbar signal","Averages of 5 vary much less than single values, so the Xbar limits are tighter and a shift in the mean shows up sooner"],answer:3,hint:"Standard error of the mean = sigma divided by the square root of n: larger subgroups → tighter limits → more sensitive to mean shifts.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A subgroup average varies less than a single value: its spread is $\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$. The Xbar limits are therefore much closer to the centre line than the I-chart limits."},{title:"Apply to the output",body:"A moderate shift in the mean around orders 86–95 stays inside the wide I-chart limits but pushes the averages of subgroups 18 and 19 past the tighter Xbar limits."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"I-MR is a valid chart, just less sensitive; neither chart uses specification limits; the R chart has no failures here."}],final:"Xbar limits are tighter → more sensitive to mean shifts → answer **D**",whyWrong:["I-MR is a valid chart for individual values; it is simply less sensitive to small shifts in the mean.","Both charts compute limits from the data, not from specifications.","The R chart shows no failures, and R values do not create Xbar signals.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531, 538"},{id:"sv-5-240",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement BEST describes **common-cause** variation?",options:["Variation from one identifiable source that appears only now and then","Variation caused by a single operator’s mistake on one day","Variation built into the process, coming from many small sources and present all the time","Variation that always appears as a point outside the control limits"],answer:2,hint:"Common cause = inherent, many small sources, always present; special cause = assignable, intermittent.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Common-cause** variation is the natural noise of a stable process: many small sources acting all the time, giving a random scatter around the average. The process should **not** be adjusted for it."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A single identifiable, intermittent source is a special cause. Points beyond the limits signal special causes, not common causes."}],final:"Common cause = inherent, always present → answer **C**",whyWrong:["An identifiable, intermittent source is a special cause.","A one-off mistake is an assignable (special) cause.","","A point beyond the limits is the classic sign of a special cause."],bookRef:"Book pp. 517–519"},{id:"sv-5-241",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Three shifts collect **repair time** for a new I-MR chart. Investigation shows the day shift measures from ticket creation, the evening shift from dispatch, and the night shift from technician arrival. Which step of the book’s control-chart set-up was skipped?",options:["Selecting the type of chart","Deciding the measurement method and criteria, and writing instructions so everyone collects the data the same way","Setting the sampling frequency","Calculating the control limits"],answer:1,hint:"Control chart set-up includes: decide the measurement method/criteria, write instructions for all phases, train the people.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s 13-step set-up includes **deciding the measurement method and criteria**, **writing instructions** for all phases, and **training** the people who collect data."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'Each shift uses a different start point for "repair time", so the same chart mixes three different measures. A clear operational definition, written and trained, is missing.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The chart type (I-MR) is fine; frequency and limits cannot fix data that mean different things."}],final:"Missing: a common measurement method and instructions → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The chart type is not the issue; any chart would mix three different definitions.","","More or fewer samples do not make the definitions agree.","Limits computed from inconsistent data are meaningless however they are calculated."],bookRef:"Book pp. 525–527"},{id:"sv-5-242",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"When unsure how often to sample, the book advises sampling more often at first, confirming that shifts are detected, and then reducing the frequency. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"If unsure, sample more often, then reduce once the process stays in control.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says: if in doubt, **sample more often**, make sure shifts are detected, then reduce the rate once the process stays in control. At the start, take extra samples."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The statement repeats that advice, so it is true."}],final:"Start frequent, then reduce → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This is the book’s own advice — start with frequent sampling and reduce later."],bookRef:"Book pp. 528–529"},{id:"sv-5-243",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these steps from the book’s control-chart set-up "cookbook" in the correct order:

- **P** — Select the type of chart
- **Q** — Select the characteristic (critical X or CTQ)
- **R** — Set the sampling interval or frequency
- **S** — Train the people
- **T** — Set the basis for subgrouping`,options:["Q → P → T → R → S","Q → T → P → R → S","Q → R → T → P → S","S → Q → T → P → R"],answer:1,hint:"Characteristic → subgrouping → chart type → frequency and sample size → … → instructions → training.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s 13 steps run: select the characteristic (1), purpose (2), collection points (3), **basis for subgrouping (4)**, **type of chart (5)**, measurement method (6), **sampling frequency (7)**, sample size (8) … instructions (12), **train the people (13)**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Q (1) → T (4) → P (5) → R (7) → S (13)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Subgrouping comes before the chart type, frequency comes after both, and training is the last step."}],final:"Q → T → P → R → S → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Puts the chart type before the subgrouping basis; the book decides subgroups first.","","Sets the frequency before the subgroups and chart type are known.","Training comes last, after everything it covers has been decided."],bookRef:"Book pp. 525–527"},{id:"sv-5-244",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"An order audit finds that one customer order has **three wrong fields**. How should this order be counted?",options:["As three defective units","As one defect","As three defective units and three defects","As one defective unit that contains three defects"],answer:3,hint:"Defective = a whole unit that fails acceptance; defect = one failed criterion. A unit can have many defects.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **defective** is a whole unit that fails acceptance, whatever its number of defects. A **defect** is one failed criterion; one unit can have several."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"One order (one unit) with three wrong fields = **1 defective** containing **3 defects**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The number of defectives can never exceed the number of units inspected."}],final:"1 defective, 3 defects → answer **D**",whyWrong:["There is only one unit, so there can be at most one defective.","Three fields failed, so there are three defects, not one.","Counts the single order three times as a defective.",""],trap:"Defects → C or U chart; defectives → NP or P chart.",bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-245",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"The book lists several uses of control charts. All of the following are among them EXCEPT:",options:["Establishing baseline performance","Evaluating a measurement system","Comparing a process before and after a change","Proving that the process meets the customer’s specification limits"],answer:3,hint:"Control charts show stability (voice of the process); specification limits are not part of a control chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists uses such as finding **baseline** performance, evaluating **measurement systems**, comparing processes and comparing **before and after** a change — and of course detecting special causes."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A control chart contains no specification limits, so it cannot prove the process meets them. That is the job of a capability study."}],final:"Meeting specs is a capability question → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Baseline performance is one of the listed uses.","Evaluating a measurement system is one of the listed uses.","Before-and-after comparison is one of the listed uses.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 516–517"},{id:"sv-5-246",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following chart choices are correct EXCEPT:",options:["Defects counted on a constant area of opportunity → C chart","Defective units with a sample size that varies → P chart","Continuous data in subgroups of 12 → Xbar-R chart","Continuous data, one value per batch → I-MR chart"],answer:2,hint:"Continuous: n = 1 → I-MR; 2–5 → Xbar-R; 10+ → Xbar-S. Attribute: defects C (constant) / U (varies); defectives NP (constant) / P (varies).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For continuous data the book’s tree goes by subgroup size: 1 → I-MR, 2–5 → Xbar-R, **10 or more → Xbar-S**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Subgroups of 12 belong on an **Xbar-S** chart; the range uses only the largest and smallest values and wastes information in big subgroups."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The C, P and I-MR choices all match the selection table."}],final:"n = 12 → Xbar-S, not Xbar-R → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Correct: defects with a constant area of opportunity → C chart.","Correct: defectives with varying n → P chart.","","Correct: single values per batch → I-MR."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-247",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'For each repair job, the team can record either **"repaired within 4 h? yes/no"** or the **actual repair time in minutes**. Which is BETTER as the basis for the control chart, and why?',options:["The actual repair time on a variables chart, because continuous data carry more information and show shifts sooner","Yes/no on a p chart, because it matches the 4-hour SLA","Yes/no on a p chart, because attribute charts are more sensitive","Either; both charts give the same information"],answer:0,hint:"Prefer continuous data where possible: it carries more information than pass or fail.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s advice: **prefer continuous data** when you can — it carries more information."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A drift from 2.5 h to 3.5 h is visible at once in repair time, but yes/no data show nothing until jobs actually cross 4 h."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"An SLA is a specification, not a reason to lose information; attribute charts need larger samples and are less sensitive."}],final:"Continuous repair time is better → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Matching the SLA throws away information; the SLA can still be reported separately.","Attribute charts are less sensitive and need larger samples than variables charts.","Yes/no keeps only one bit of the information the minutes contain."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-248",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team wants to start an Xbar-R chart of **call-quality scores** next week. The latest measurement-system study shows:

\`\`\`
Gage R&R Study – Call-quality score (assessors)
Source               %Study Var
Total Gage R&R          38.4
  Repeatability         12.1
  Reproducibility       36.4
Part-to-Part            92.3
Number of Distinct Categories = 3
\`\`\`

What should the team do?`,options:["Start the chart; 38.4% is acceptable because it is below 50%","Start the chart, but improve the scoring form, because repeatability is the main problem","Start the chart; 3 distinct categories is acceptable because it is at least 2","Fix the measurement system first — mainly the assessor-to-assessor differences — because the chart would largely track measurement noise"],answer:3,hint:"Gage R&R %Study Var: <10% good, 10–30% marginal, >30% unacceptable; ndc ≥ 5.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"%Study Var for the gage is 38.4% (> 30%, unacceptable) and ndc = 3 (< 5). Reproducibility (36.4%) is far larger than repeatability (12.1%)."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"A control chart is only as good as its data. With this much measurement error, signals and limits would reflect assessor differences, not the process."},{title:"Decide",body:"Calibrate the assessors (shared standards, training, scoring examples), repeat the study, and then start the chart."}],final:"Fix reproducibility before SPC → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Over 30% %Study Var is unacceptable; 50% is not the threshold.","Reproducibility (between assessors), not repeatability, dominates here.","The rule is ndc ≥ 5; 3 categories cannot separate the process well.",""],trap:"Reproducibility = differences between appraisers; repeatability = the same appraiser repeating.",bookRef:"Book pp. 516–517"},{id:"sv-5-249",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'To keep the handle-time chart "clean", an analyst leaves out every call that was transferred to second-line support before forming the subgroups. What does the book say about this?',options:["It is wrong: the sample must represent the process as it normally runs, and leaving out part of the output biases the chart and makes it invalid","It is right: difficult calls are outliers and should always be removed before limits are calculated","It is acceptable as long as the exclusion is written in the chart notes","It only matters for attribute charts, not for variables charts"],answer:0,hint:"Sample while the process runs normally; any special treatment or bias makes the chart invalid.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says the process must **run normally** while you sample, and any special treatment or bias makes the chart **invalid**. The sample must represent the population."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Transferred calls are part of the real process. Removing them makes the process look faster and more stable than it is, and hides any change in transfer behaviour."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Points are removed only for a documented, assignable cause — not whole categories in advance. Writing the bias down does not remove it."}],final:"Excluding part of the output = biased chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Normal, frequent cases are not outliers; removing them in advance biases the data.","Documenting a bias does not make the chart valid.","Bias invalidates any chart, variables or attribute."],bookRef:"Book pp. 517–519"},{id:"sv-5-250",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Given the choice, which subgrouping approach does the book say most people prefer?",options:["Larger subgroups taken less often","One large daily subgroup taken at the busiest hour","As many units as possible in a single weekly subgroup","Smaller subgroups taken more often"],answer:3,hint:"Most people take smaller subgroups more frequently rather than large subgroups rarely.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says that, given the choice, most people take **smaller subgroups more often** rather than large ones rarely — frequent samples catch a shift sooner, so the customer suffers less."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Large, rare subgroups (daily or weekly) mix conditions and leave long gaps in which a shift goes unseen."}],final:"Small and frequent → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Large, infrequent subgroups leave long gaps where a shift is missed.","A single daily sample misses changes during the rest of the day.","A weekly subgroup mixes many conditions and reacts far too slowly.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 528–529"},{id:"sv-5-251",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"When a control chart signals a special cause, what does the chart **itself** tell you?",options:["The root cause of the change","Which specification limit has been exceeded","That the process has changed and roughly when — but not why","How much the process must be adjusted to bring it back"],answer:2,hint:"SPC detects special-cause variation; finding the cause needs process knowledge and root-cause tools.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: SPC tells you the process is **out of control, but not why**. The chart shows that something changed and when."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The team must use process knowledge, the response plan and root-cause tools to find the reason."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Specifications are not on a control chart, and the chart gives no adjustment amount."}],final:"When, not why → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The chart flags a change; it never names the cause.","Specification limits are not part of a control chart.","","The chart does not calculate an adjustment; adjusting without a cause is tampering."],bookRef:"Book pp. 516–517"},{id:"sv-5-252",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"According to the book, sampling frequency is a balance between which two things?",options:["The subgroup size versus the number of control limits","The cost of sampling and testing versus the cost of missing a shift (when the customer suffers)","The specification width versus the control-limit width","The number of analysts versus the number of charts"],answer:1,hint:"Frequency trades the cost of sampling against the cost of missing an out-of-control condition.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book frames sampling frequency as balancing the **cost of sampling and testing** against the **cost of missing a shift** — if a shift is missed because sampling is too rare, the customer suffers."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Specification width and staffing do not set the sampling interval; the subgroup size is decided before the frequency."}],final:"Cost of sampling vs cost of a missed shift → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Subgroup size is decided first; the number of limits is fixed by the chart type.","","Specifications are not used when designing a control chart.","Staffing is a practical constraint, not the book’s balance."],bookRef:"Book pp. 528–529"},{id:"sv-5-253",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Phase I I-MR chart of **daily outage minutes** has just been built:

\`\`\`
I-MR Chart of Daily Outage Minutes (Phase I, 50 days)
Test Results for I Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  4, 9, 13, 22, 30, 41, 47
Causes identified so far: none
\`\`\`

The manager suggests deleting all seven points and recalculating the limits. What is the BEST response?`,options:["Delete all seven and recalculate, because Phase I limits must come from in-control data","Keep the chart as it is and calculate capability, because seven points out of 50 is acceptable","Do not delete them: the process is not yet stable; investigate and remove the special causes first, and only exclude points whose causes are found and documented","Widen the limits to 4 sigma so that fewer points fail"],answer:2,hint:"In Phase I, exclude only points with an identified, documented and corrected special cause.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Seven Test 1 failures in 50 days, with no causes identified — the process is clearly unstable."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"Points are removed from the limit calculation only when their **assignable cause** is found and dealt with. Deleting unexplained points narrows the limits artificially and hides real problems."},{title:"Decide",body:"Investigate the signals, remove the causes, collect new data, and only then set the limits. Capability is meaningless until the process is stable."}],final:"Unstable process → find causes before setting limits → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Deleting points without known causes produces limits that are too tight and hide the instability.","Capability needs a stable process; seven unexplained signals mean it is not stable.","","Widening limits hides signals instead of finding their causes."],bookRef:"Book pp. 525–527"},{id:"sv-5-254",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Five call recordings taken at the same moment from five different contact-centre sites form a rational subgroup. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"A rational subgroup = units made under very similar conditions in a short time, from one stream.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **rational subgroup** is a small sample made under very similar conditions in a short time — for example five consecutive items from one machine. The book: five items made at the same time on **different machines** are **not** rational."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Five sites are five different streams, so site-to-site differences would sit inside the subgroup and inflate the limits."}],final:"Different streams in one subgroup → not rational → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Same time is not enough; the units must come from the same conditions (one stream).",""],trap:'Trap: "same moment" sounds rational, but different sources make it irrational.',bookRef:"Book pp. 528–529"},{id:"sv-5-255",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An I-MR chart of **daily average queue wait (s)** in a stable period shows $\\bar{x}=42.0$ s and $\\overline{MR}=3.0$ s. Constants for a moving range of 2: $E_2=2.66$, $D_4=3.267$, $d_2=1.128$. What is the **UCL of the Individuals chart**?",options:["51.00 s","49.98 s","51.80 s","45.38 s"],answer:1,hint:"$$UCL_X=\\bar{x}+E_2\\overline{MR}$$ with E₂ = 2.66.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\bar{x}=42.0$, $\\overline{MR}=3.0$, $E_2=2.66$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$UCL_X=\\bar{x}+E_2\\overline{MR}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$UCL_X=42.0+2.66\\times 3.0=42.0+7.98=49.98$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"$E_2=\\dfrac{3}{d_2}=\\dfrac{3}{1.128}=2.66$, so the limit is three short-term sigmas above the centre line."}],final:"UCL = 49.98 s → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Used 3 × MR̄; the moving range must first be converted to sigma, which is what E₂ = 2.66 does.","","Used D₄ = 3.267, which belongs to the MR chart’s UCL, not the I chart.","Multiplied by d₂ = 1.128 instead of E₂; d₂ is a divisor that converts MR̄ to sigma."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-256",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A lab pull-tests **one** fibre-connector sample from each production batch until it breaks (a destructive test). Which chart should track pull strength?",options:["Xbar-R chart","p chart","u chart","I-MR chart"],answer:3,hint:"One value per batch, destructive testing → I-MR.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists **destructive testing** as a classic I-MR situation: you cannot test many units, so each point is a single value."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"One continuous measurement per batch → subgroup size 1 → I-MR."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Xbar-R needs several units per subgroup; p and u charts are for attribute data, not strength measurements."}],final:"Destructive, one per batch → I-MR → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Xbar-R needs 2–5 units per subgroup; destroying more units is costly.","A p chart needs pass or fail data and wastes the strength measurement.","A u chart counts defects per unit; strength is continuous.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-257",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab prints:

\`\`\`
I-MR Chart of Daily Order Backlog (orders)
I Chart:   UCL = 186.3   CL = 170.0   LCL = 153.7
MR Chart:  MR-bar = 6.13
Descriptive Statistics:  N = 40   Mean = 170.0   StDev = 14.8
\`\`\`

A colleague asks why the limits are only ±16.3 from the centre line instead of ±3 × 14.8 = ±44.4. Which answer is correct?`,options:["The I chart estimates sigma from the moving range (about 5.4, short-term); the overall StDev of 14.8 is much larger, which suggests the level drifted or shifted over the 40 days","Minitab has made an error; the limits should use the overall StDev of 14.8","The I chart uses three standard errors of the mean, which are smaller than the StDev","The I chart uses ±2.66 standard deviations instead of ±3"],answer:0,hint:"$$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{\\overline{MR}}{d_2}$$ (short-term); the overall StDev also includes shifts between periods.",steps:[{title:"Compute the short-term sigma",body:"$$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{\\overline{MR}}{d_2}=\\dfrac{6.13}{1.128}=5.43$$ so $3\\hat{\\sigma}=16.3$ — exactly the distance to the limits."},{title:"Compare with the overall StDev",body:"The overall StDev (14.8) is almost three times larger. Short-term (neighbour-to-neighbour) variation is small, so the extra spread must come from the level moving over time."},{title:"Interpret",body:"This is deliberate: using the overall StDev would widen the limits and **hide** the drift. The tight limits will likely flag it."}],final:"Limits use MR̄ divided by d₂, not the overall StDev → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Minitab is right; the overall StDev would inflate the limits and hide shifts.","Standard errors of a mean apply to Xbar charts; here n = 1.","Trap: 2.66 multiplies MR̄, not sigma; the limits are still three sigmas."],trap:"E₂ = 2.66 multiplies MR̄; the limits are always ±3 sigma.",bookRef:"Book pp. 518, 538"},{id:"sv-5-258",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab prints:

\`\`\`
I-MR Chart of Jitter (ms)
Test Results for MR Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  6, 18, 27, 33
Test Results for I Chart
No test failures.
\`\`\`

What should the analyst conclude?`,options:["The process is in control, because the I chart shows no failures","The MR chart can be ignored, because only the I chart shows the process level","Delete the four points and recalculate the limits so that both charts are clean","Short-term variation is unstable; investigate the MR signals first, because the I-chart limits are built from MR̄ and cannot be trusted yet"],answer:3,hint:"Read the variation chart (MR) first; the I-chart limits come from MR̄.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Four MR points are beyond 3 sigma: there are sudden jumps between neighbouring values. The I chart shows nothing."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:'The book: look at the **MR chart first** — if short-term variation is unstable, the I-chart limits are not reliable. "No failures" on the I chart is therefore no reassurance.'},{title:"Decide",body:"Investigate what caused the big jumps (for example route changes or load spikes) before judging the level."}],final:"MR unstable → investigate MR first → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A clean I chart means little when the MR̄ behind its limits is unstable.","The MR chart tracks short-term variation, which is often where the problem shows first.","Deleting points without known causes hides instability.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 518, 538"},{id:"sv-5-259",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"An I-MR chart is built from **k** individual values. How many moving ranges are there?",options:["k","k ÷ 2","k + 1","k − 1"],answer:3,hint:"Each moving range uses two consecutive points; the first point has no predecessor.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each moving range is $MR_i=|x_i-x_{i-1}|$, the difference between two **consecutive** points. The first value has no previous value, so there is no MR for it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"k values → **k − 1** moving ranges, and $\\overline{MR}$ divides the sum by k − 1."}],final:"k − 1 moving ranges → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The first value has no predecessor, so there is one fewer MR than values.","Moving ranges overlap (each point is used twice); they are not formed in separate pairs.","There cannot be more moving ranges than values.",""],trap:"Dividing the sum of MRs by k instead of k − 1 is a common calculation slip.",bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-260",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab prints for **daily fibre-repair time (h)**:

\`\`\`
I-MR Chart of Repair Time (h)
Test Results for I Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  19
Data row 19:  45.0   (all other values between 3.6 and 5.4)
\`\`\`

What should the team do FIRST?`,options:["Start a root-cause project on why repairs took ten times longer that day","Delete row 19 and recalculate the limits without investigating","Widen the limits, because one extreme value shows the process is more variable than thought","Check whether the value is real — a value of 45.0 among values of about 4–5 looks like a data-entry slip (for example 4.50); correct the record if so"],answer:3,hint:"When a chart signals, first confirm that the data point is real before acting on the process.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"One point is about ten times larger than all others — the typical look of a misplaced decimal point (4.50 typed as 45.0)."},{title:"Apply the idea",body:"A signal can come from the **measurement or recording**, not the process. Confirm the raw record (ticket timestamps) first."},{title:"Decide",body:"If it is an entry error, correct it and note the cause (and consider a validation rule). If it is real, then investigate the process."}],final:"Verify the data before reacting → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A full root-cause effort on a likely typo wastes time; verify first.","Deleting without checking hides a possible real event and leaves the cause unknown.","Limits are not changed because of one extreme point.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-261",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"The Individuals chart uses the constant $E_2=2.66$ in $\\bar{x}\\pm E_2\\overline{MR}$. Where does 2.66 come from?",options:["It is the D₄ constant for subgroups of two","It is the z-value that leaves 0.5% in each tail","It is the A₂ constant for subgroups of five, used because MR̄ comes from pairs","It is 3 divided by d₂ = 1.128, so the limits are three short-term sigmas from the centre line"],answer:3,hint:"$$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{\\overline{MR}}{d_2}$$, so 3 sigma = 3 × MR̄ ÷ d₂.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Sigma is estimated from the moving range: $\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{\\overline{MR}}{1.128}$. Three sigmas are therefore $\\dfrac{3}{1.128}\\overline{MR}=2.66\\,\\overline{MR}$."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"D₄ = 3.267 is for the MR chart; the z for 0.5% per tail is 2.576; A₂ for n = 5 is 0.577."}],final:"E₂ = 3 ÷ 1.128 = 2.66 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["D₄ for n = 2 is 3.267 and sets the MR chart’s UCL.","The 0.5%-per-tail z is 2.576 — close to 2.66 but unrelated.","A₂ for n = 5 is 0.577; it belongs to the Xbar-R chart.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-262",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following statements about the **MR chart** of an I-MR pair are true EXCEPT:",options:["Each moving range is the absolute difference between two consecutive values","Its upper control limit is 3.267 × MR̄","Its centre line is the standard deviation of all the individual values","Its lower control limit is 0"],answer:2,hint:"MR chart: CL = MR̄, UCL = D₄·MR̄ = 3.267·MR̄, LCL = D₃·MR̄ = 0.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The MR chart plots $MR_i=|x_i-x_{i-1}|$. Its centre line is **MR̄**, the average moving range; UCL = 3.267 × MR̄; LCL = 0 because D₃ = 0 for ranges of two."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The centre line is not a standard deviation of all values — that statement is the false one."}],final:"CL of the MR chart = MR̄ → answer **C**",whyWrong:["True: that is the definition of a moving range.","True: D₄ = 3.267 for ranges of two points.","","True: D₃ = 0, so the LCL is 0."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-263",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"An Xbar-R chart of **fault-diagnosis time (min)** uses subgroups of **4 consecutive tickets** and $\\bar{R}=6.0$ min. Constants: n = 2: D₄ = 3.267; n = 4: A₂ = 0.729, D₃ = 0, D₄ = 2.282; n = 5: D₄ = 2.114. What is the **UCL of the R chart**?",options:["12.68 min","13.69 min","19.60 min","4.37 min"],answer:1,hint:"$$UCL_R=D_4\\bar{R}$$ with D₄ taken for the subgroup size n.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"n = 4, $\\bar{R}=6.0$, D₄ (n = 4) = 2.282."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$UCL_R=D_4\\bar{R}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$UCL_R=2.282\\times 6.0=13.69$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"LCL = D₃ × R̄ = 0. Any subgroup range above 13.69 min signals a change in short-term spread."}],final:"UCL_R = 13.69 min → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Used D₄ for n = 5 (2.114) instead of n = 4.","","Used D₄ for n = 2 (3.267), the moving-range constant.","Used A₂ (0.729), which belongs to the Xbar chart, not the R chart."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-264",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab prints:

\`\`\`
Xbar-R Chart of Router Provisioning Time (min)    n = 5
Test Results for R Chart:   No test failures.
Test Results for Xbar Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  17     (Xbar = 21.4, LCL = 23.9)
\`\`\`

Subgroup 17 was **faster** than the lower control limit. What should the team do?`,options:["Treat it as a special cause: find out what was different — if it is a real improvement, learn from it and standardize it; if it is a recording error, correct it","Nothing — a point below the LCL is good news for provisioning time","Delete subgroup 17 and recalculate the limits","Widen the Xbar limits so that fast subgroups no longer signal"],answer:0,hint:"A point beyond either limit is a special cause — favourable signals are investigated too.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"The R chart is stable, so the Xbar limits are valid. Subgroup 17’s mean (21.4) is below the LCL (23.9)."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"A point beyond **either** limit is a special cause. A favourable one is a chance to learn: something made the process faster."},{title:"Decide",body:"Investigate. If it is real (for example a new script or an experienced engineer), make it standard; if it is a data error, correct it."}],final:"Favourable signal → investigate and learn → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Ignoring a favourable signal wastes the chance to find and standardize the cause.","Deleting without investigation throws away the evidence.","Widening limits hides special causes instead of explaining them."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-265",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab prints:

\`\`\`
Xbar-R Chart of Install Duration (h)    n = 4
Test Results for R Chart
TEST 2. 9 points in a row on same side of center line.
Test Failed at points:  36, 37, 38, 39     (all above center line)
Test Results for Xbar Chart:   No test failures.
Note: four new installers joined the crew before subgroup 28.
\`\`\`

Which interpretation is BEST?`,options:["The average install duration has shifted upwards since subgroup 28","The within-subgroup spread has increased since about subgroup 28, probably linked to the new installers; investigate the spread before trusting the Xbar chart","Nothing has changed, because no point is beyond the R-chart limits","The Xbar chart shows no failures, so the R-chart result can be ignored"],answer:1,hint:"R chart = short-term spread; Xbar chart = level. Read the R chart first.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Nine or more ranges in a row above the R-chart centre line from about subgroup 28 — a sustained increase in **spread**, not in the average."},{title:"Link to the note",body:"The timing matches the four new installers: mixed experience inside each crew sample increases the range."},{title:"Decide",body:'Investigate the spread (training, standard work). The R chart must be in control before the Xbar chart can be trusted, so "no Xbar failures" is not reassurance.'}],final:"R chart Test 2 → spread increased → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A signal on the R chart is about spread; the Xbar chart (level) shows no failures.","","Test 2 is a special-cause signal even with no point beyond the limits.","The Xbar limits depend on R̄, so an unstable R chart makes them unreliable."],bookRef:"Book pp. 538, 548–552"},{id:"sv-5-266",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book want the process **level** (Xbar chart) and the **spread** (R chart) tracked separately?",options:["Because a change in level and a change in spread usually have different causes and need different corrective actions","Because the R chart is used to set the specification limits","Because only the R chart is sensitive to shifts in the mean","Because averaging subgroups removes the need to look at spread"],answer:0,hint:"Xbar = level, R = spread; they need different corrective actions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Xbar chart monitors the **level** (between-subgroup changes) and the R chart the **short-term spread** (within-subgroup). The book notes they usually need **different corrective actions**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Neither chart sets specifications; the Xbar chart, not the R chart, detects mean shifts; and averaging hides spread, which is why the R chart is needed."}],final:"Different causes, different actions → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Specification limits come from the customer, never from a control chart.","Mean shifts appear on the Xbar chart; the R chart tracks spread.","Averages hide the spread, which is exactly why a separate R chart is kept."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-267",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"In the book’s Xbar-R example, each hourly subgroup takes **one hole from each of five different drill presses**. Under what condition is that subgrouping acceptable?",options:["Always, because five machines make the subgroup more representative","Only if the presses are known to perform alike; otherwise press-to-press differences inflate R̄, hide signals, and each press needs its own chart","Only if the subgroup size is at least 10","Only if the specification limits are wide"],answer:1,hint:"Rational subgroup = one stream under very similar conditions; units from different machines are normally not rational.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s rational-subgroup rule says items made at the same time on **different machines are not** a rational subgroup — differences between machines sit inside the subgroup and inflate the range."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The drill-press example works only on the assumption that there is little difference between the presses. If that is not known, use a separate chart per press."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Representativeness is not the goal of a subgroup; subgroup size and specifications do not fix mixing."}],final:"Acceptable only if the presses are alike → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Mixing streams widens the limits and buries real signals.","","A larger subgroup would mix the streams even more.","Specification limits play no part in rational subgrouping."],bookRef:"Book pp. 528–529, 548–551"},{id:"sv-5-268",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"An Xbar-R chart with subgroups of **n = 5** shows **LCL = 0** on the R chart. Why?",options:["Because the process has no short-term variation","Because D₃ is 0 for subgroup sizes up to 6, so the R chart has no lower limit","Because the R chart always has an LCL of 0, whatever the subgroup size","Because R̄ is small compared with the subgroup averages"],answer:1,hint:"$$LCL_R=D_3\\bar{R}$$ and D₃ = 0 for n ≤ 6.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The R-chart lower limit is $LCL_R=D_3\\bar{R}$. For subgroup sizes of 6 or less, **D₃ = 0**, so the LCL is 0."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"With n = 5, D₃ = 0 → LCL = 0, whatever the value of R̄."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"From n = 7 upwards D₃ is positive, so the LCL is not always 0; the LCL has nothing to do with how big R̄ is compared with the averages."}],final:"D₃ = 0 for n ≤ 6 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["R̄ is greater than 0 here; the LCL is 0 because of the constant, not because spread is zero.","","For n ≥ 7, D₃ is positive and the R chart does have a lower limit.","R̄ is multiplied by D₃ = 0, so its size makes no difference."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-269",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt is asked to interpret a new Xbar-R chart. Put the steps in a sensible order:

- **P** — Check the Xbar chart for points beyond the limits and run-test patterns
- **Q** — Check that the R chart is in control
- **R** — Investigate any signals using the response plan and record the causes
- **S** — Confirm that the subgroups are rational and the data are correct`,options:["S → P → Q → R","S → Q → P → R","Q → S → P → R","P → Q → S → R"],answer:1,hint:"Valid data first; then R chart; then Xbar chart; then act on signals.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A chart built on wrong data or irrational subgroups is invalid, so check that first. The Xbar limits come from R̄, so the **R chart is read before the Xbar chart**. Action follows the reading."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"S (data and subgroups) → Q (R chart) → P (Xbar chart) → R (investigate signals)."}],final:"S → Q → P → R → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Reads the Xbar chart before confirming the R chart, whose R̄ sets the Xbar limits.","","Judges the R chart before confirming the data behind it are valid.","Starts with the Xbar chart and only later checks the data and the R chart."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-270",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A u chart of **wiring defects per home installation** has $\\bar{u}=0.8$. Today **20** installations were audited and **30** defects were found. Which conclusion is correct?",options:["u = 1.50 is below today’s UCL of 3.48 → no signal","u = 0.67 is below today’s UCL of 1.40 → no signal","u = 1.50 is above today’s UCL of 1.40 → special-cause signal","u = 1.50 is above the centre line, but one point cannot signal until nine are on the same side"],answer:2,hint:"$$u_i=\\dfrac{c_i}{n_i}\\qquad UCL_i=\\bar{u}+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{u}}{n_i}}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\bar{u}=0.8$, $n_i=20$ installations, $c_i=30$ defects."},{title:"Plotted point",body:"$$u_i=\\dfrac{c_i}{n_i}=\\dfrac{30}{20}=1.50$$"},{title:"Today’s limit",body:"$$UCL_i=\\bar{u}+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{u}}{n_i}}=0.8+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.8}{20}}=0.8+3\\times 0.2=1.40$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"1.50 > 1.40 → a Test 1 special-cause signal for today."}],final:"u = 1.50 > UCL 1.40 → signal → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Forgot to divide ū by nᵢ inside the square root (0.8 + 3 × √0.8).","Divided installations by defects (20 ÷ 30) instead of defects by installations.","","Mixes up the tests: one point beyond 3 sigma (Test 1) is a signal on its own."],bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-271",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A u chart tracks **defects per cabinet** in street-cabinet audits, with $\\bar{u}=0.50$. Each day’s UCL has already been calculated for its own sample size:

| Day | Cabinets audited | Defects | UCL |
|---|---|---|---|
| Mon | 50 | 36 | 0.800 |
| Tue | 8 | 9 | 1.250 |
| Wed | 32 | 31 | 0.875 |
| Thu | 18 | 16 | 1.000 |

On which day(s) is there a Test 1 signal?`,options:["Wednesday only","Tuesday only","Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday","Monday only"],answer:0,hint:"$$u_i=\\dfrac{c_i}{n_i}$$ and compare each day with its own UCL.",steps:[{title:"Compute each point",body:"$$u_{Mon}=\\dfrac{36}{50}=0.72\\quad u_{Tue}=\\dfrac{9}{8}=1.125\\quad u_{Wed}=\\dfrac{31}{32}=0.969\\quad u_{Thu}=\\dfrac{16}{18}=0.889$$"},{title:"Compare with each day’s own UCL",body:"Mon 0.72 < 0.800; Tue 1.125 < 1.250; **Wed 0.969 > 0.875**; Thu 0.889 < 1.000."},{title:"Decide",body:"Only Wednesday signals. Tuesday has the highest u, but its small sample gives it the widest limit."}],final:"Only Wednesday is above its own UCL → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Picked the highest u without using Tuesday’s own (wide) limit for n = 8.","Compared every day with Monday’s tight limit of 0.800 instead of each day’s own limit.","Picked the day with the most defects (36) without converting counts to defects per unit."],trap:"With varying n, each point has its own limits — never compare with one fixed line.",bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 540"},{id:"sv-5-272",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A u chart tracks **defects found per kilometre** of fibre route surveyed. Surveyors cover between **3 and 12 km** a day. Which statement about this chart is correct?",options:["The unit is one survey day, so the sample size is always 1 and the limits are constant","A c chart is better, because the defects are counted","The unit is one kilometre; each day’s sample size is the km surveyed that day, so the limits widen on short-survey days and narrow on long ones","A p chart is better, because the sample size varies"],answer:2,hint:'U chart: u = defects ÷ units; the "unit" (area of opportunity) can be any defined size; limits depend on each nᵢ.',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The unit (search area) is whatever you define — a page, a form, a kilometre. The U chart plots defects **per unit** and its limits use each subgroup’s $n_i$."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Unit = 1 km, so $n_i$ = km surveyed that day (3 to 12). $$UCL_i=\\bar{u}+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{u}}{n_i}}$$ is wider when $n_i$ is small."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A c chart needs a constant area of opportunity; a p chart is for defective units, not defect counts."}],final:"Unit = 1 km; limits vary with km surveyed → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Treating each day as one unit ignores that the area inspected changes daily.","A c chart assumes the same area every time; here it varies from 3 to 12 km.","","Varying n alone does not make it a p chart; these are defects, not defectives."],bookRef:"Book pp. 540–541"},{id:"sv-5-273",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Why is the centre line of a u chart calculated as **total defects ÷ total units** rather than the average of the daily u values?",options:["Because days with more units inspected must carry more weight; averaging the daily rates gives a small day the same weight as a large one","Because the daily u values cannot be calculated when n varies","Because the average of the daily rates is always larger than the true rate","Because Minitab cannot average ratios"],answer:0,hint:"$$\\bar{u}=\\dfrac{\\text{total defects}}{\\text{total units inspected}}$$",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The centre line is a **weighted** rate: $$\\bar{u}=\\dfrac{\\sum c_i}{\\sum n_i}$$ A day with 60 units holds much more information than a day with 5."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Daily u values can always be computed; the simple average may be higher or lower than the weighted rate — it is not always larger; software limits are irrelevant."}],final:"Totals weight each day by its size → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Each daily u is easy to compute (defects ÷ units).","The simple average can be higher or lower; the problem is wrong weighting, not direction.","This is a statistics rule, not a software limitation."],bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-274",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`An analyst runs a P chart on configuration-review data and Minitab stops with:

\`\`\`
P Chart of Configuration Errors
* ERROR * Number of events must be less than or equal to the number of trials.
Row 4:  Trials (configs reviewed) = 25    Events (errors found) = 31
\`\`\`

What is the real problem?`,options:["Row 4 is a typing error and should be deleted","An NP chart should be used, because the data are counts","The data are defects — one configuration can contain several errors — so a U chart of errors per configuration should be used","An I-MR chart of the error counts should be used"],answer:2,hint:"Defects (several per unit possible) → C or U; defectives (one per unit at most) → NP or P.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:'In row 4 there are 31 errors in 25 configurations — more "events" than "trials". A proportion defective can never exceed 1.'},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"That is only possible when a unit can have **several defects**. These are defect counts, not defectives, and the number reviewed varies → **U chart**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Deleting the row hides the real data type; NP has the same at-most-one-per-unit limit as P; an I-MR of raw counts ignores the varying sample size."}],final:"More defects than units → U chart → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The row is valid data; it simply shows several errors per configuration.","NP also counts defectives and has the same restriction as P.","","Raw counts on an I-MR chart ignore that the number of configurations reviewed varies."],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 540"},{id:"sv-5-275",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A p chart of **misrouted calls** uses daily samples of **n = 50** calls with $\\bar{p}=0.02$. What is the **LCL**?",options:["−0.0394","0","0.0002","0.0794"],answer:1,hint:"$$LCL=\\bar{p}-3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}{n}}$$ and a negative LCL is set to 0.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\bar{p}=0.02$, n = 50."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$LCL=\\bar{p}-3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}{n}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$LCL=0.02-3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.02\\times 0.98}{50}}=0.02-3\\times 0.0198=0.02-0.0594=-0.0394$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"A proportion cannot be negative, so the LCL is set to **0**. A day with zero misrouted calls cannot signal as unusually good with n = 50."}],final:"LCL = 0 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Correct arithmetic, but a negative LCL must be set to 0.","","Forgot to multiply the standard error by 3.","This is the UCL (p̄ + 3 standard errors), not the LCL."],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-276",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"table",difficulty:1,stem:`A team considers four metrics for an attribute control chart:

| Metric | What is recorded each day |
|---|---|
| W | Orders with at least one error, out of the orders checked (number checked varies) |
| X | Total errors found, out of the orders checked (number checked varies) |
| Y | Minutes late for each technician visit |
| Z | Number of alarms on the same fixed cluster of 40 cell sites |

For which metric is a **P chart** the BEST choice?`,options:["X","Y","W","Z"],answer:2,hint:"P chart = proportion defective (each unit good or bad), sample size may vary.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A P chart plots the **proportion of defective units** — each unit is either good or bad — and handles a varying sample size."},{title:"Apply to the table",body:"W classifies each order as with-error or not (defective) and n varies → **P chart**. X counts errors (defects) per order → U chart. Y is continuous → I-MR. Z counts defects on a constant area → C chart."}],final:"Defective orders with varying n → P chart → answer **C**",whyWrong:["X counts all errors (defects), and one order can have several → U chart.","Y is continuous data → a variables chart such as I-MR.","","Z counts defects on a constant area of opportunity → C chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 531, 539"},{id:"sv-5-277",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Daily sample sizes vary from 60 to 600. An analyst calculated **one UCL from the average sample size (n = 400)** and used it for every day:

\`\`\`
P Chart of Failed Activations      P-bar = 0.050
Day     n     Failed      P       UCL (n = 400 for all days)
 3     60        7      0.117    0.0827   <- flagged
 4    600       47      0.078    0.0827
\`\`\`

What is the correct conclusion?`,options:["The chart is right: Day 3 is a signal and Day 4 is not","Neither day is a signal, because both proportions are close to the average limit","Both days are signals, because both proportions are above p̄","Each day needs limits based on its own n: Day 3 (UCL ≈ 0.134) is not a signal, but Day 4 (UCL ≈ 0.077) is"],answer:3,hint:"$$UCL_i=\\bar{p}+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}{n_i}}$$ uses each subgroup’s own nᵢ.",steps:[{title:"Spot the error",body:"With varying sample sizes, P-chart limits must use each day’s own $n_i$, not one average n."},{title:"Recalculate",body:"$$UCL_3=0.05+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.05\\times 0.95}{60}}=0.05+0.084=0.134$$ $$UCL_4=0.05+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.05\\times 0.95}{600}}=0.05+0.027=0.077$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Day 3: 0.117 < 0.134 → no signal. Day 4: 0.078 > 0.077 → **signal**. The fixed limit gave both verdicts the wrong way round."}],final:"Use each day’s n: Day 4 signals, Day 3 does not → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Accepts limits built on an average n; that is wrong when n varies.","Ignores that Day 4’s large sample gives a much tighter limit, which it exceeds.","Being above the centre line alone is not a Test 1 signal.",""],trap:"Small samples → wide limits; large samples → tight limits.",bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 539"},{id:"sv-5-278",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Apart from being the average proportion defective, what does the book say the **centre line** of a P chart represents?",options:["The customer’s specification for the defect rate","The process capability for that attribute","The management target for the process","The maximum defect rate the process is allowed"],answer:1,hint:"P-chart centre line = average proportion defective = process capability for the attribute.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For a P chart, the book says the centre line $\\bar{p}$ — the average proportion defective — is also your **process capability** for that attribute."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Specifications, targets and maximum allowed rates come from the customer or management; none of them is drawn on a control chart."}],final:"p̄ = attribute capability → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Specifications are not part of a control chart.","","Targets are business goals, not the voice of the process.","A maximum allowed rate is a specification, not the process average."],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-279",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement matches the book’s guidance for setting up a **P chart**?",options:["Five subgroups are enough if each has more than 500 units","Use at least 20 subgroups to calculate the limits; samples are usually larger than 50","The sample size must be the same for every subgroup","Plot the number of defectives rather than the proportion"],answer:1,hint:"P chart: at least 20 subgroups; samples usually > 50; n may vary; plot the proportion.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book recommends **at least 20 samples** before calculating P-chart limits, and notes P-chart samples are usually **larger than 50**. The sample size may vary."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Large subgroups do not replace the need for enough subgroups; a constant n and plotting the count describe the NP chart."}],final:"At least 20 subgroups, n usually > 50 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Few subgroups give unreliable limits however large each one is.","","The P chart is designed to handle varying sample sizes.","Plotting the number defective describes an NP chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 539"},{id:"sv-5-280",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team charts the proportion of orders completed **right first time** (a "good" proportion, so higher is better):

\`\`\`
P Chart of Right-First-Time Orders      n = 200 per day
P-bar = 0.880     UCL = 0.949     LCL = 0.811
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  27     (P = 0.790)
\`\`\`

Which interpretation is correct?`,options:["Day 27 is good news, because a point below the LCL always means fewer defects","Day 27 is an unfavourable special cause: the right-first-time rate fell below the LCL, so it should be investigated","Day 27 is not a signal, because P charts only test the upper limit","Day 27 is common-cause variation, because 0.790 is still above 0.75"],answer:1,hint:'Check what the proportion measures: when the chart tracks a "good" outcome, a drop below the LCL is bad.',steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"The chart plots the proportion of **good** orders. Day 27 (0.790) is below the LCL (0.811) — a Test 1 failure."},{title:"Interpret the direction",body:'For a "good" proportion, falling below the LCL means **more** orders needed rework that day — an unfavourable special cause.'},{title:"Decide",body:'Investigate day 27 through the response plan. Always read what the plotted proportion measures before deciding whether "low" is good or bad.'}],final:'Below LCL on a "good" proportion = worse → answer **B**',whyWrong:["Here the proportion measures good orders, so lower means worse.","","Both limits apply; a point below the LCL is a Test 1 signal.","A point beyond a control limit is special cause; 0.75 is an invented threshold, not a limit."],trap:'Know whether the chart plots defectives or "goods" before reading a low point.',bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 539"}];export{e as items};
